// SECURITY — VERIFIED CAPABILITY ESCAPE. Currently EXPLOITABLE.
//
// This program declares NO capabilities. It compiles and runs under the
// deny-by-default `inferred` mode AND passes `--strict-capabilities`.
// It recovers a secret string byte-for-byte and dereferences memory 4096
// bytes past a pointer it was handed, without faulting.
//
// Actual output:
//   recovered: TOPSECRET-APIKEY
//   far read did not fault: false
//
// WHY THIS IS THE MOST SERIOUS OPEN BUG: Kryos is sold on capability
// attenuation for agent tooling -- a tool is handed exactly the authority it
// needs and no more. `str_to_ptr` + `ptr_byte_at` defeats that completely: a
// tool granted ZERO capabilities can read any string the host process holds,
// including API keys, tokens, and other tools' arguments. Every
// capability-attenuation guarantee in the ecosystem projects
// (kryos-mcp-governed, kryos-injection-guard, kryos-fs-jail, the audit trail)
// rests on a boundary this walks straight through.
//
// The raw-memory builtins -- alloc, free_bytes, ptr_read_i64, ptr_write_i64,
// ptr_byte_at, ptr_set_byte, str_to_ptr, arr_to_ptr -- require no capability
// and are not confined to `unsafe`. They are treated as internal runtime
// plumbing, which is true of how the STDLIB uses them and false of what a
// user program can do with them.
//
// WHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINE FIX (measured, not assumed): the stdlib depends on
// these heavily. `alloc` appears in 14 stdlib modules, `ptr_read_i64` in 7,
// and `str_to_ptr` in crypto/db/fs/io/net. Adding them to
// `required_capability_for_builtin` cascades: `std::fs` would start demanding
// `ffi` from every caller. Closing this properly needs a trusted-computing-base
// distinction -- stdlib internals may use raw memory, user code may not --
// rather than a table entry.
//
// ACCEPTANCE: this file must FAIL to compile with a capability error, while
// `tests/conformance/*` and the stdlib continue to build unchanged.

fn main() {
  let secret = "TOPSECRET-APIKEY"
  let sp = str_to_ptr(secret)
  // walk memory from a pointer we were handed, at arbitrary offsets
  let mut i = 0
  let mut out = ""
  while i < 16 {
    out = out + char_from(ptr_byte_at(sp, i))
    i = i + 1
  }
  println("recovered: " + out)
  // read a far offset we never allocated
  let far = ptr_read_i64(sp, 4096)
  println("far read did not fault: " + to_string(far != far))
}
